{
  "arm": "synonyms-relater",
  "calls": [
    {
      "selected_hints": [
        {
          "id": "sha256:110263c600dd6be855b5e8cc0c09bc086660a77cdcf70ac17e5099fcdb9c6fa1",
          "source_id": "ORIGINAL-0001",
          "path": "original-0001.html",
          "hash": "sha256:93d03d3493443fc88a7926765b45deac1cfc15592e9bf03cc27b5a2c737fe124",
          "body_start": 1522,
          "text": "Churchill and Roosevelt told Stalin that the invasion “would be possible” by August 1, 1944, but that no decision had yet been made to name a supreme commander. To this latter point, Stalin pointedly rejoined, “Then nothing will come of these operations. Who carries the moral and technical responsibility for this operation?” Churchill and Roosevelt acknowledged the need to name the commander without further delay. Shortly after the conference ended, Roosevelt appointed Gen. Dwight David Eisenhower to that position.",
          "body_projection": "body-text-no-headings-v1",
          "context_truncated": false,
          "retrieval_score": 4,
          "sorter_node_id": "node:ee2c973cc87f23bb700fa5d5bd9270ca91d9c4b2a53446d3b708aaa848f8dfd6",
          "sorter_basis": "lexical_ranker_original_location"
        },
        {
          "id": "sha256:ccd09ad5b28134606c8f0e4688817c97e1867efb6179f9871f6920b56ea45632",
          "source_id": "ORIGINAL-0001",
          "path": "original-0001.html",
          "hash": "sha256:93d03d3493443fc88a7926765b45deac1cfc15592e9bf03cc27b5a2c737fe124",
          "body_start": 2535,
          "text": "Against a tense backdrop of uncertain weather forecasts, disagreements in strategy, and related timing dilemmas predicated on the need for optimal tidal conditions, Eisenhower decided before dawn on June 5 to proceed with Overlord. Later that same afternoon, he scribbled a note intended for release, accepting responsibility for the decision to launch the invasion and full blame should the effort to create a beachhead on the Normandy coast fail.",
          "body_projection": "body-text-no-headings-v1",
          "context_truncated": false,
          "retrieval_score": 4,
          "sorter_node_id": "node:4e996480caad39c21312c620e7f7dc5b89adc27f4aeb05f606e81743b29f5510",
          "sorter_basis": "lexical_ranker_original_location"
        },
        {
          "id": "sha256:be892d418a38d4414daf4b2af0bc200fcb5d045a08314d8c3041ee40d1b18cd1",
          "source_id": "ORIGINAL-0002",
          "path": "original-0002.html",
          "hash": "sha256:2b2900e5cc6ad6fbcf9027310ff7ea41568a67df84a51a8f72352e1945ef85a4",
          "body_start": 349,
          "text": "Citation: Act creating Yellowstone National Park, March 1, 1872; Enrolled Acts and Resolutions of Congress, 1789-1996; General Records of the United States Government; Record Group 11; National Archives. Photograph, Cascade Creek, Yellowstone, 1869-1878 by William H. Jackson; Records of the U.S. Geological Survey; Record Group 57; National Archives.",
          "body_projection": "body-text-no-headings-v1",
          "context_truncated": false,
          "retrieval_score": 4,
          "sorter_node_id": "node:b21cf5e4f673f3fdce2c3000755879adc8326686ae6b9d8ac3787fac9f5bd8f3",
          "sorter_basis": "lexical_ranker_original_location"
        },
        {
          "id": "sha256:5f7441c70c778020d9f98c32cc1993ff2747041ed135db2a5648f461907a468d",
          "source_id": "ORIGINAL-0003",
          "path": "original-0003.html",
          "hash": "sha256:0b85c918a8ebb763e38521d95e79e8d65d80d3c5d2667c14d2992a8d13e92b69",
          "body_start": 770,
          "text": "Initially, the Civil War between North and South was fought by the North to prevent the secession of the Southern states and preserve the Union. Even though sectional conflicts over slavery had been a major cause of the war, ending slavery was not a goal of the war.",
          "body_projection": "body-text-no-headings-v1",
          "context_truncated": false,
          "retrieval_score": 4,
